{"name":"Light Stargate","hex":"#c7d2dd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"stargate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hypothetical device consisting of a traversable portal (typically a wormhole) that can send one to another location light years away nearly instantaneously."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stargate"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stargate","blocks":[{"text":"Stargate is a military science fiction media franchise owned by Amazon MGM Studios. It is based on the film directed by Roland Emmerich, which he co-wrote with producer Dean Devlin; production company StudioCanal owns the rights to the original film. The franchise is based on the idea of an alien wormhole device that enables nearly instantaneous travel across the cosmos. The franchise began with the film Stargate, released on October 28, 1994, by Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer and Carolco, which grossed US$197 million worldwide. In 1997, Brad Wright and Jonathan Glassner created a television series titled Stargate SG-1 as a sequel to the film. This show was joined by Stargate Atlantis in 2004, Stargate Universe in 2009, and a prequel web series, Stargate Origins, in 2018. Also consistent with the same story are a variety of books, video games and comic books, as well as the direct-to-DVD movies Stargate: Children of the Gods, Stargate: The Ark of Truth, and Stargate: Continuum, which concluded the first television show after 10 seasons."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stargate","wikidata":"Q11927"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/90/Sg1stargatefront.jpg/330px-Sg1stargatefront.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/90/Sg1stargatefront.jpg/330px-Sg1stargatefront.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An activated Stargate, the central object of the Stargate universe, here depicted in the SG-1 television series.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASg1stargatefront.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/53/Sg1-poster.jpg/250px-Sg1-poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/Sg1-poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/53/Sg1-poster.jpg/250px-Sg1-poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The original starring cast of Stargate SG-1.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASg1-poster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b8/SGUcast.jpg/330px-SGUcast.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut